WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.289 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  foreign  [Applause]  Bus and Rail strikes may have been  causing chaos today  [Applause]  but it's action by nurses that's proving  painful for Rishi sunak has that been  your favorite event today on a visit to  Northern Ireland he again ruled out  reopening negotiations over pay we've  got enormous gratitude for all our  public sector workers for the job they  do I know things are difficult right now  that's why the government is providing  lots of support for people particularly  with energy bills and it's why the  government accepted in full the  recommendations of an independent pay  review body about what appropriate and  fair pay levels were but within his own  party there is a shift I do hope that a  way can be found to chart a middle  course with 2x cabinet Minister's  calling for compromise between the 19  rise nurses want and the four percent  offered I think 19 is unaffordable and  inflation busting pay Rises of that  nature will only exacerbate a vicious  cycle which doesn't help anybody but at  the same time the nurse's case is one  that I think we all understand and  therefore finding is solution to this  problem which can only then help deal  with the backlog and allow everybody to  get back to the work that I know they  want to do seems to me to be a  reasonable course of action a formatory  health Minister has a similar plea the  government does need to find the Nexus  strategy for this and that probably  means referring it back to the pay  review body given that we are mid-year  on what the pay review body has already  recommended this could probably be  looking with a promise to next year's  settlement and in return the strikes  would be called off  polls suggest two-thirds of Voters back  the nurses but that doesn't seem to be  changing Minds in Whitehall the  government's position is at least  consistent they argue their offer is  fair and they don't have money for more  whilst labor say they talk but so far  haven't promised more cash yet there are  more than seven million people already  on Hospital waiting lists in England  yesterday up to 60 percent of routine  operations were canceled in areas with  industrial action and it's all likely to  get far worse next week  nurses are due to strike again on  Tuesday ambulance workers on Thursday  and if the deadlock continues it's  patients who will suffer Joe Pike Sky  News

